<GuideLayout
  title="Quilt Backing and Binding"
  description="How to calculate quilt backing yardage with overhang and how to figure binding strip counts from perimeter."
  path="/guides/quilt-backing-and-binding/"
  answerFirst="Backing should extend beyond the quilt top—often 4 inches per side for longarm quilting—then be pieced if the quilt is wider than your fabric. Binding length is the perimeter plus about 12 inches for joins; divide by usable fabric width to get strip count."
>
  <h2>Size the quilt first</h2>
  <p>
    Use the <a href="/tools/quilt-size-calculator/">quilt size calculator</a> from mattress width, drop, and pillow tuck.
    Standard mattress sizes are listed under <a href="/reference/mattress-sizes/">mattress sizes</a>.
  </p>
  <h2>Backing</h2>
  <p>
    Add overhang, then decide vertical vs horizontal seams. Wide-back fabrics can eliminate seams for many queens.
    Run numbers in the <a href="/tools/quilt-backing-calculator/">backing calculator</a>.
  </p>
  <h2>Binding</h2>
  <p>
    Double-fold binding strips are commonly cut 2.25–2.5 inches wide on straight grain for straight edges (bias for curves).
    The <a href="/tools/quilt-binding-calculator/">binding calculator</a> returns strips and yardage.
  </p>
  <h2>HSTs and blocks</h2>
  <p>
    Planning pieced tops? The <a href="/tools/half-square-triangle-calculator/">HST calculator</a> covers traditional, 2-at-a-time, and 8-at-a-time cutting.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
